1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,893
You've built great tools for Claude Code: a hook, a subagent, a skill. Now your teammate wants them too.

2
00:00:07,343 --> 00:00:12,033
Copying files into every project by hand? There's a better way: plugins.

3
00:00:12,483 --> 00:00:17,914
I'll explain what a plugin is, when to use one, and then we'll build one and share it, step by step.

4
00:00:19,464 --> 00:00:20,399
So, what is a plugin?

5
00:00:20,849 --> 00:00:28,244
A plugin bundles skills, subagents, hooks, and MCP servers into one package, that installs as one unit.

6
00:00:28,694 --> 00:00:33,900
A small file called a manifest, plugin dot JSON, gives it a name and a description.

7
00:00:34,350 --> 00:00:40,936
And a marketplace is a catalog, usually a Git repository, where people find your plugin and install it by name.

8
00:00:42,386 --> 00:00:44,633
Here's the whole journey, in three parts.

9
00:00:45,083 --> 00:00:49,403
First, build: put your components in one folder, with a manifest.

10
00:00:49,853 --> 00:00:54,718
Second, list it in a marketplace: a small catalog file in a Git repository.

11
00:00:55,168 --> 00:01:00,530
Third, install: one command, and every component in the plugin loads in Claude Code.

12
00:01:00,980 --> 00:01:06,422
Each skill and subagent gets the plugin's name as a prefix, so nothing clashes with your own tools.

13
00:01:07,872 --> 00:01:08,955
So when do you need a plugin?

14
00:01:09,405 --> 00:01:17,389
Skills, subagents, hooks, and MCP servers all work on their own. If it's one tool, just for you, you don't need a plugin.

15
00:01:17,839 --> 00:01:25,143
Use a plugin when you want several pieces packaged together: to share with teammates, reuse in many projects, or ship updates.

16
00:01:26,593 --> 00:01:28,274
Our example: a team kit.

17
00:01:28,724 --> 00:01:38,949
In earlier videos, we built a hook that protects your secrets, a test-runner subagent, and a YouTube description skill. Sharing them means copying files, project by project.

18
00:01:39,399 --> 00:01:45,859
We'll pack all three into one plugin, called team-kit, so anyone can install everything with a single command.

19
00:01:47,409 --> 00:01:49,206
Step one: create the plugin.

20
00:01:49,656 --> 00:01:55,481
I'm in my project. I make a folder called team-kit, with a dot claude plugin folder inside it.

21
00:01:55,931 --> 00:01:59,063
Inside, the manifest: plugin dot JSON.

22
00:02:01,131 --> 00:02:07,745
The name is required. It becomes the prefix on every skill and subagent in the plugin. No spaces.

23
00:02:08,195 --> 00:02:13,804
The description is what people see when they browse plugins. And the version lets you release updates.

24
00:02:14,254 --> 00:02:18,782
Only plugin dot JSON goes in that hidden folder. Everything else goes next to it.

25
00:02:19,532 --> 00:02:21,478
Step two: add the components.

26
00:02:21,928 --> 00:02:26,602
Each type has its own folder: agents, hooks, scripts, and skills.

27
00:02:27,052 --> 00:02:32,399
I copy in the three tools from earlier videos: the subagent, the hook script, and the skill.

28
00:02:33,461 --> 00:02:40,810
The hook needs one file: hooks, slash hooks dot JSON. It's the same format as the hooks section in a settings file.

29
00:02:41,260 --> 00:02:47,783
One difference: the script path starts with the plugin root variable. It always points to wherever the plugin is installed.

30
00:02:48,633 --> 00:02:51,048
Step three: test it, before sharing.

31
00:02:51,498 --> 00:02:55,953
First, claude plugin validate checks the manifest and every component.

32
00:02:56,403 --> 00:03:01,583
Then I load it for one session with the plugin dir flag. No install needed while you're building.

33
00:03:02,033 --> 00:03:05,883
Let's try the hook. I ask Claude to add a comment to my dot env file.

34
00:03:06,333 --> 00:03:11,226
Blocked, by the hook inside the plugin. Now, the subagent: run the tests.

35
00:03:11,676 --> 00:03:16,930
Claude delegates to team-kit colon test-runner. Notice the plugin's name, as a prefix.

36
00:03:17,380 --> 00:03:23,643
And the skill is ready too, as slash team-kit colon youtube-description. Everything works. Time to share it.

37
00:03:24,493 --> 00:03:27,187
Step four: share it, with a marketplace.

38
00:03:27,637 --> 00:03:34,933
A marketplace is just a folder with a catalog file, and your plugins inside. I create one, and copy team-kit into it.

39
00:03:35,383 --> 00:03:40,925
The catalog is marketplace dot JSON: a name, an owner, and the list of plugins.

40
00:03:42,668 --> 00:03:48,357
Each plugin entry has a name, and a source: the path to the plugin, from the marketplace folder.

41
00:03:48,807 --> 00:03:53,780
I validate it, then push the my-marketplace folder to GitHub, as its own repository.

42
00:03:54,230 --> 00:04:00,923
Now, any teammate runs two commands. Add the marketplace, using the GitHub owner and repository name.

43
00:04:01,373 --> 00:04:05,507
Then install the plugin: its name, at, the marketplace name.

44
00:04:05,957 --> 00:04:11,425
That's it. The hook, the subagent, and the skill, in every project, from one install.

45
00:04:12,975 --> 00:04:15,877
Step five: go further. Here are four tips.

46
00:04:16,327 --> 00:04:23,853
One: type slash plugin in Claude Code. The Discover tab lists plugins from Anthropic's official marketplace, ready to install.

47
00:04:24,303 --> 00:04:34,665
Two: check the cost. An enabled plugin adds its descriptions to every session. Claude plugin details shows how many tokens. Our team-kit adds about ninety.

48
00:04:35,115 --> 00:04:41,092
Three: only install plugins you trust. A plugin runs code with your permissions, so review it first.

49
00:04:41,542 --> 00:04:46,920
Four: when you improve your plugin, bump the version in the manifest, and your teammates get the update.

50
00:04:48,470 --> 00:04:49,124
Let's recap.

51
00:04:49,574 --> 00:04:55,298
A plugin packages skills, subagents, hooks, and MCP servers into one unit.

52
00:04:55,748 --> 00:05:01,929
You build it with a plugin dot JSON manifest and a folder per component, and test it with the plugin dir flag.

53
00:05:02,379 --> 00:05:07,013
And you share it through a marketplace, so anyone can install the whole kit with one command.

54
00:05:08,463 --> 00:05:14,677
The team-kit plugin and the marketplace from this video are linked in the description. Copy them, and make them yours.

55
00:05:15,127 --> 00:05:21,958
If this helped, like the video, and subscribe to Zero Cost AI for more free AI tutorials. See you in the next one!

